  "provider_query": "English Wikipedia HTTP article: the default TCP port",
  "answer": "The English Wikipedia **HTTP** article states that unencrypted HTTP over TCP typically uses **port 80**.\n\n1. **HTTP – Wikipedia** — TCP/IP connections use well-known ports, “typically port 80 if the connection is unencrypted or port 443 if the connection is encrypted.” Before HTTP/3, the usual transport is TCP; HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP\n\n2. **Port (computer networking) – Wikipedia** — “By default, HTTP uses port 80, and HTTPS uses port 443.” Port 80 is listed for HTTP on the World Wide Web.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_(computer_networking)\n\n3. **List of TCP and UDP port numbers – Wikipedia** — Port **80** is assigned to Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP); HTTP/1.x and HTTP/2 use TCP. HTTPS is on **443**.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_TCP_and_UDP_port_numbers\n\n4. **Port 80 – Wikipedia** — Disambiguation page: TCP port 80 is most often used by HTTP.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_80\n\n5. **IANA Service Name and Port Number Registry** — `http` / `www` on **80/tcp** is “World Wide Web HTTP” (reference RFC 9110).  \n   https://www.iana.org/assignments/service-names-port-numbers\n\n6. **MDN Glossary: Port** — Default port for HTTP is 80; default for HTTPS is 443.  \n   https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Port\n\n7. **Super User** — Tim Berners-Lee’s early HTTP 0.9 text assumed port 80 when none was specified; RFC 1340 (1992) assigned `www`/`http` to 80/tcp.  \n   https://superuser.com/questions/996828/why-was-port-80-chosen-as-the-default-http-port-and-443-as-the-default-https-por\n\n8. **Stack Overflow** — Default port for HTTP is 80 (443 for HTTPS); the port is omitted from a URL when it is the scheme default.  \n   https://stackoverflow.com/questions/50888270/why-do-we-need-to-specify-a-port-number-while-using-http-protocol",
